Z||000026||Seanh||11-06-2000||09:56 PM||||I agree that putting a front engine in a Fiero defeats the purpose. It really doesnt make me all the angry, granted I wouldnt be caught dead in that thing, but I dont have a problem with somone else doing it. After all, it is his car. I emailed him about listing it as an 88, when obviously it was not. He emailed me back and stated that is was originally an 85, but after the conversion it had all the 85 components  replaced with 88 components on it, there for making it an 88. <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">   <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Raydar||11-08-2000||11:32 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>...was originally an 85, but after the conversion it had all the 85 components  replaced with 88 components on it, there for making it an 88. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Now, <I>that</I> would be a neat trick. <BR>And then he bolted up the 13" wheels that won't fit an 88, anyway. Right.<BR>Me thinks the guy has eaten too many magic mushrooms.<P>I apologize for dredging this up, again. I promise to let it die in peace.<BR>Won't make any more comments.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-08-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000028||Boomtastic||11-10-2000||08:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BHoldwick:<BR><B>Ok once you take the engine out of the back of the fiero you just F*ck*d up the whole consept. Does it make me mad hell yea if you want to make a car a street rod choose a sorry base like a camero. If you all agree let me get a O hell yea!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you research the "concept" of the Fiero, you would see that Pontiac had originally thought about placing the engine up front. They (obviously) decided against it. If they had done it, it would have been a front wheel drive piece of crap. I do it - with the engine in the right way, retaining the rear wheel drive - and you say you're angry over it. It can be done tastefully. The one on eBay is another story .... Hot rodding has always been about improving upon your vehicle. If that means simply taking out your motor and replacing it with a larger one, so be it. In my case, I didn't want to mate up a 700hp Chevy 383 to a weak transaxle (sorry Archie - no offense!) knowing that it would not take the abuse I would be subjecting it too.||||reg||5
